      "notes": "All claims are adequately supported by their cited evidence. E9 is from immigrationbarrister.co.uk (a secondary media source citing Migration Observatory) \u2014 this is a mild provenance concern but not blocking since the underlying data is attributed to the Migration Observatory. E12 regional figures are from immigrationbarrister.co.uk alone; again a secondary source concern but the figures are plausible and the claim is modest. E22 is correctly tiered as projected and attributed to critics. The direction 'improves' is well-supported: multiple credible sources (MAC, Migration Observatory, Commons Library data) show the raised threshold disproportionately excludes lower earners, women, and those outside London/South-East, so reversing it has a clear inequality-narrowing direction. Magnitude 'moderate' is justified given the narrow slice of migration routes affected. The rationale references E28 ('70% of the entire working population') which is not in the claims array \u2014 this is a minor concern (unanchored factual assertion in rationale) but the same figure is broadly consistent with E9/E29 evidence. No lever_leakage, no party_blind issues, no false_balance."
